WEBVTT

00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:01.000
Silent Service.

00:00:01.380 --> 00:00:05.520
The world is deep at war, and you're submerged among the enemy.

00:00:20.900 --> 00:00:29.300
And welcome to NEStalgia, a chronological exploration of every NES game released in North America.

00:00:29.300 --> 00:00:30.300
I'm Mike.

00:00:30.520 --> 00:00:31.400
I'm Sean.

00:00:31.920 --> 00:00:36.320
And I'm Joe. Guys, what's your submarine experience like?

00:00:36.320 --> 00:00:39.060
Has anybody been on a submarine, done any naval time?

00:00:41.880 --> 00:00:43.960
I've heard of the Sub-Mariner.

00:00:45.180 --> 00:00:49.300
Namor? Who I believe is a superhero.

00:00:50.400 --> 00:00:56.620
Correct. I saw the hunt for Red October when I was very young.

00:00:57.920 --> 00:01:05.440
And I think there's a submarine docked in Lake Erie in Buffalo.

00:01:05.440 --> 00:01:06.740
So I've seen one.

00:01:06.740 --> 00:01:14.080
But no, I've never done a tour of service on a submarine.

00:01:14.500 --> 00:01:28.920
So I was actually in like a theatrical production, or like a radio play, reenactment of a piece of literature about a game called Schoon.

00:01:31.300 --> 00:01:33.920
Oh, yeah. And who did I play in that again?

00:01:34.160 --> 00:01:38.900
I was like the captain that was asking for Narrakeen's help.

00:01:38.900 --> 00:01:40.180
And that was you, Sean.

00:01:40.300 --> 00:01:43.220
You were Narrakeen. That may have been me.

00:01:43.420 --> 00:01:45.360
That was Sean. I may have been Narrakeen, yes.

00:01:45.600 --> 00:01:49.080
And you feel like that prepped you enough, Joe, for playing this game?

00:01:49.520 --> 00:01:50.780
Yeah. What's the difference?

00:01:50.780 --> 00:02:04.840
Well, Schoon is a submarine shmup, but that didn't really like launch a genre of submarine shmups that was just kind of like a standalone thing where it was like, hey, instead of a spaceship, let's just make it a submarine underwater, which is a great idea.

00:02:04.960 --> 00:02:19.320
Don't get me wrong. But this game and this whole series, Silent Service, pretty much launched the submarine simulator video game genre, which in this day, they're actually, you know, like there's a lot of different simulators in general, right?

00:02:19.360 --> 00:02:20.980
We got a PC building simulator.

00:02:20.980 --> 00:02:25.780
So it really shouldn't be a surprise that there's a whole genre for submarine simulators.

00:02:25.920 --> 00:02:31.160
But it goes all the way back to 1985 with Silent Service.

00:02:31.160 --> 00:03:01.140
And if anybody's interested in submarine simulation games, whether they're retro or modern, I went on subsim.com, where you can really get like an enhanced understanding of these kinds of games with people who either like have actually been on submarines and understand this shit for real, or people who are just like enthusiasts, just like there's people who like use Microsoft Flight Simulator, and then never actually fly a real plane, but they can totally fly a Microsoft Flight Simulator.

00:03:02.540 --> 00:03:07.920
There's people who like never had any submarine experience, but they're really into these submarine simulators.

00:03:08.680 --> 00:03:12.520
Wow, that's a that's a lot of background that you've given us already.

00:03:14.060 --> 00:03:23.020
I didn't know that there was more than a handful of submarine simulators to call it a genre and not like just a series.

00:03:23.500 --> 00:03:31.120
Right, right. Like it's not just Silent Service, like other even in this day and age, I believe there's still like five ongoing submarine simulators.

00:03:31.140 --> 00:03:33.740
That are competing with each other for the audience.

00:03:34.640 --> 00:03:36.820
Over those couple dozen people.

00:03:37.820 --> 00:03:44.480
I guess I get it because it's anything that's like a flight simulator, which is like, I guess a little more mainstream.

00:03:45.040 --> 00:03:47.860
I'd say that's more mainstream compared to submarines.

00:03:47.860 --> 00:04:02.780
Submarines. Well, my point is that like submarines have like a similar amount of or if not way more like in-depth controls and like elements you get factors you have to be aware of.

00:04:02.900 --> 00:04:09.120
It's just that the flight simulator element is like just a sexier version of that because you're flying, I think in most people's heads.

00:04:10.140 --> 00:04:21.520
Yeah, I think it's a lot cooler to like fly over your like the city that you live in than it is to like, oh, wow, did you catch the Marianas Trench DLC or something like that?

00:04:21.680 --> 00:04:24.060
Like, I think maybe that's cooler.

00:04:24.060 --> 00:04:36.300
And more so than maybe like more appealing for us, the interesting thing about this game isn't that it launched a submarine simulator genre or, you know, span of video games and a fan forum and everything.

00:04:36.480 --> 00:04:38.820
Is that for us, it's a Sid Meier game.

00:04:39.360 --> 00:04:41.620
We like Sid Meier games a lot.

00:04:41.820 --> 00:04:47.080
And this is our first one that we're playing on the on the podcast and kind of exploring.

00:04:47.300 --> 00:04:54.500
So rather than talk about Sid Meier and all of his history and everything, I felt like maybe we should talk about what we like about Sid Meier games.

00:04:54.900 --> 00:04:57.340
Obviously, he's made a lot of different kinds of games.

00:04:57.340 --> 00:05:06.840
They all kind of fall into this simulation or strategy, more strategy than simulation, I would say, area.

00:05:07.280 --> 00:05:12.840
But, you know, different historical periods, different types of things that you're in control of.

00:05:13.600 --> 00:05:15.300
Sean, what do you like about Sid Meier games?

00:05:15.300 --> 00:05:28.620
Well, Civilization was probably the first strategy game that I ever played and sort of birthed my love for strategy games of all shapes and sizes.

00:05:29.600 --> 00:05:34.400
It was actually the Super NES version of Civilization.

00:05:35.000 --> 00:05:40.600
And then that that turned into kind of forgetting about it for a while.

00:05:40.600 --> 00:05:44.500
And then Civilization 4 and then 5 and then 6.

00:05:45.180 --> 00:05:50.080
I never got into Pirates, but I always wanted to because that seemed pretty cool.

00:05:51.220 --> 00:05:57.560
But when it came out, I don't think I had like a computer that could run it or something along those lines.

00:05:57.760 --> 00:06:00.940
But yeah, big fan of Sid Meier.

00:06:01.320 --> 00:06:09.400
Yeah, as far as I mean, at least what I was thinking while I was playing this game was I was inevitably comparing it to Pirates,

00:06:09.400 --> 00:06:11.500
which is a game that I had played on the NES.

00:06:11.500 --> 00:06:13.520
I think I played it for the first time with you, Mike, right?

00:06:13.620 --> 00:06:17.160
We just were randomly playing like we were just on a retro pie.

00:06:17.500 --> 00:06:19.280
Yeah. We picked it out of nowhere.

00:06:19.560 --> 00:06:20.780
I didn't realize it was a Sid Meier game.

00:06:22.660 --> 00:06:24.580
And and we had a blast with it.

00:06:24.640 --> 00:06:25.780
And then I've played it a lot since.

00:06:28.400 --> 00:06:41.860
But this kind of I mean, there's definitely a lot of differences, but this reminds me of it in a lot of ways where it's like this early attempt at very in-depth simulation.

00:06:41.860 --> 00:06:50.820
Although it is I feel like they go in different directions where this gets really, really this gets really, really deep with one core.

00:06:52.280 --> 00:07:02.900
Gameplay aspect of, you know, piloting the submarine, whereas Pirates is gets maybe not quite as deep on each like individual tiny granular level,

00:07:02.900 --> 00:07:07.600
but it gets very broad with like everything you can do as like the life of a pirate.

00:07:08.380 --> 00:07:11.800
It's also sort of simulating a like a cartoon version.

00:07:13.080 --> 00:07:16.440
Yeah. I mean, at least with pirates, I mean.

00:07:16.620 --> 00:07:23.000
Yeah. There is like some historical accuracy as far as like the time periods and stuff.

00:07:23.140 --> 00:07:27.700
Yeah. I mean, you're you're you're you're definitely like swashbuckling a lot more than I imagine.

00:07:27.920 --> 00:07:30.860
Actual pirates. I don't think they swashbuckled quite as much.

00:07:31.840 --> 00:07:43.040
And the idea of like auteur theory for filmmaking, you know, with the great directors who kind of no matter what genre or type of film they make, you feel their stamp on it.

00:07:43.260 --> 00:07:46.580
That's something that's not so common in video games.

00:07:46.880 --> 00:07:53.420
I think even like no one thinks of Shigeru Miyamoto as like an auteur because it's just like that's just Nintendo.

00:07:53.760 --> 00:07:59.900
And like, yeah, he's kind of touching everything that happens at Nintendo, but he's not like putting his specific mark on things.

00:07:59.900 --> 00:08:01.520
I don't know if that makes sense or not.

00:08:01.620 --> 00:08:07.220
But like, you know, there's not many guys who are known in this industry for making like specific games over and over.

00:08:07.380 --> 00:08:14.940
Kojima comes to mind as somebody who definitely like is renowned for like, oh, well, he's the one who made the game.

00:08:15.100 --> 00:08:19.440
So it's like he's very closely involved with the touch and feel of things.

00:08:19.440 --> 00:08:28.180
Sid Meier does kind of feel like one of those, though, where it's like, yeah, probably by now he's just like putting his name on the branding of the game and not directly involved in it.

00:08:28.280 --> 00:08:34.700
But you know what you're you know, what kind of experience you're getting when it says Sid Meier presents blah, blah, blah.

00:08:34.900 --> 00:08:37.100
Like, yeah, you kind of get the feel for it.

00:08:37.160 --> 00:08:39.160
Right. I guess so.

00:08:39.160 --> 00:08:46.400
I wouldn't say that it's it's the same like the same kind of a tour ship as a Kojima.

00:08:46.580 --> 00:09:01.540
Like when I think of that, that term, I think like what you're getting is like a collection of like, yes, the same ideas, but also quirks and like sort of a warts and all sort of thing.

00:09:02.760 --> 00:09:24.560
And when I think of more with like Sid Meier, I would I would actually put them more into a like a Chris Avalone or a what's the nerdy guy with Doom, that dude, the Doom guy or like a Gabe Newell from back in the day when he made games.

00:09:24.560 --> 00:09:35.520
Like I'd say it's more so like when you hear about a good director's movie coming up, not so much a Quentin Tarantino when you like know it's going to be like too cool for itself.

00:09:35.560 --> 00:09:40.680
And it's going to have the same themes where it's more so in this situation.

00:09:41.140 --> 00:09:46.900
Yeah, it might stick in the same genre, but it doesn't have the baggage that I think auteurs have.

00:09:47.200 --> 00:09:50.740
Got it. Yeah, that's a good that's a great point, too, especially where we are right now.

00:09:50.900 --> 00:09:54.460
You'll notice that this isn't called Sid Meier's silent service.

00:09:54.560 --> 00:10:02.560
This is very early in his career, not in the sense of like how many years he's been playing around with making video games.

00:10:02.680 --> 00:10:09.380
He first made a tic-tac-toe version on his own computer using, I think, basic or whatever.

00:10:09.660 --> 00:10:10.940
A tic-tac-toe simulator.

00:10:11.180 --> 00:10:12.740
Yeah, like, yeah, well, you know what I mean.

00:10:12.940 --> 00:10:14.760
Highly simulated tic-tac-toe.

00:10:14.760 --> 00:10:26.380
He was a young kid just playing around with it and made a tic-tac-toe game in 1975, then made an ASCII version of like Star Trek that was just obviously all text based.

00:10:26.380 --> 00:10:36.860
And that like kind of brought his attention, I think, because that was both mixing like a huge fan base, but with like an early computers fan base as well.

00:10:36.920 --> 00:10:41.940
So there was it was obviously not like officially licensed or whatever, but that kind of put him on the map.

00:10:42.080 --> 00:10:46.520
In 1983, he went on to make NATO Commander, which was his first strategy game.

00:10:46.520 --> 00:10:49.040
I think it's very obvious what you're doing in that game.

00:10:49.320 --> 00:10:56.200
And then in 1985, Silent Service is, you know, the submarine simulator game.

00:10:56.300 --> 00:11:02.120
And he'll go on to do like Sid Meier's Pirates, as we mentioned, in 87 and then Civilization and all that.

00:11:02.220 --> 00:11:06.800
But his name wasn't on these things like Sid Meier's Pirates is the name of the game.

00:11:06.900 --> 00:11:07.940
It's not just pirates.

